[[:db/retract 17592186220284 :answer-field/choices 17592186220288] [:db/retract 17592186220284 :answer-field/choices 17592186220289] [:db/retract 17592186220284 :answer-field/choices 17592186220285] [:db/retract 17592186220284 :answer-field/choices 17592186220286] [:db/retract 17592186220284 :answer-field/choices 17592186220287] {:db/id 17592186221433 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b82c8fbc-5979-517c-956f-f5afc90605c9" "question-02d9f6f3-7af5-52c4-b620-b99c6f0b589d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221155 :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} = F(s),$\nthen\n$\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{at}f(t)\\right\\}  = F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{t\\sin(2t) \\right\\} = \\frac{2(2)s}{(s^{2}+2^2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-5t}\\cdot t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\} & =\\frac{2(2)(s+5)}{((s+5)^{2}+{2}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(s+5)}{((s+5)^{2}+4)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221434 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4a4eb980-3664-5af2-945a-e7530601aa00" "question-b26511a8-09e9-54a7-8467-ef5beee0c7a0"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221167 :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of  $g(t) = 2t^2 + 5te^{4t}$ for $s > 4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t)\\} & =L\\{2{t}^{2}+5t{e}^{4t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{2{t}^{2}\\}+L\\{5t{e}^{4t}\\} \\\\  & =2L\\{{t}^{2}\\}+5L\\{t{e}^{4t}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s > 0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{t\\} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ L\\{{t}^{2}\\} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} = F(s),$ then $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{at}f(t)\\right\\}  = F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ te^{4t} \\right\\} = \\dfrac{1}{(s-4)^2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $g(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t)\\} & =2L\\{{t}^{2}\\}+5L\\{t{e}^{4t}\\} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+5\\cdot \\frac{1}{(s-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{5}{(s-4)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>4.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221436 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-526e692c-07a8-5c67-8d8e-821cf2ff406c" "question-b781ac41-d289-50fe-8eb5-284b3b53ff9b" "question-593b9890-14ef-5ad1-b4d2-54793d0a7bd4" "question-e41ff26b-731d-5b74-9b6c-2d6a197328e1"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221159 :question/worked-solution "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-7t}f(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-7t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+7)t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $\\displaystyle F(s) = \\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-st} \\, f(t)\\, \\textrm{d}t,$ where $s > s_0.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-(s+7)t} \\, f(t)\\, \\textrm{d}t = F(s+7),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns + 7 > s_0\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad \ns > s_0 - 7.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-b82c8fbc-5979-517c-956f-f5afc90605c9" :question/id #uuid "b82c8fbc-5979-517c-956f-f5afc90605c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-331827" :question/problem "Given that $L\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}},$ find the Laplace transform $L\\{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot {t}^{4}\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\nL\\{{t}^{4}\\}=\\frac{4!}{{s}^{5}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot {t}^{4}\\} & =\\frac{4!}{(s+3)^{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{(s+3)^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-efd7b592-4a98-55bf-97ff-8128ee4c0d28"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-efd7b592-4a98-55bf-97ff-8128ee4c0d28" :answer-field/id #uuid "efd7b592-4a98-55bf-97ff-8128ee4c0d28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ad655293-6b3b-5513-add2-ff6c5cdef0f8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ad655293-6b3b-5513-add2-ff6c5cdef0f8" "answer-698ab393-4cb8-5c51-801d-01a9a0a55b96" "answer-92467dfc-7fb3-5ffa-ba45-6e28719e9f5d" "answer-3cc1d439-9963-58cb-a205-d61ca3a432cb" "answer-177fa7ee-9b47-5b28-b20c-baed96a9d314"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad655293-6b3b-5513-add2-ff6c5cdef0f8" :answer/id #uuid "ad655293-6b3b-5513-add2-ff6c5cdef0f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{24}{(s+3)^{5}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-698ab393-4cb8-5c51-801d-01a9a0a55b96" :answer/id #uuid "698ab393-4cb8-5c51-801d-01a9a0a55b96" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{24}{(s+4)^{5}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-92467dfc-7fb3-5ffa-ba45-6e28719e9f5d" :answer/id #uuid "92467dfc-7fb3-5ffa-ba45-6e28719e9f5d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{24}{(s-3)^{5}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3cc1d439-9963-58cb-a205-d61ca3a432cb" :answer/id #uuid "3cc1d439-9963-58cb-a205-d61ca3a432cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{(s+3)^{5}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-177fa7ee-9b47-5b28-b20c-baed96a9d314" :answer/id #uuid "177fa7ee-9b47-5b28-b20c-baed96a9d314" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{6}{(s+3)^{4}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186304947 :question/problem "Given that $L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},$ find the Laplace transform $L\\{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{6}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\} & =\\frac{s+3}{(s+3)^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{s+3}{(s+3)^{2}+36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-02d9f6f3-7af5-52c4-b620-b99c6f0b589d" :question/id #uuid "02d9f6f3-7af5-52c4-b620-b99c6f0b589d" :question/math-academy-id "q-341501" :question/problem "Given that $L\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}},$ find the Laplace transform $L\\{{e}^{7t}\\cdot {t}^{5}\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\nL\\{{t}^{5}\\}=\\frac{5!}{{s}^{6}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{7t}\\cdot {t}^{5}\\} & =\\frac{5!}{(s-7)^{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{120}{(s-7)^{6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b90c7511-6cb7-52d0-a241-3716bc5adacd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b90c7511-6cb7-52d0-a241-3716bc5adacd" :answer-field/id #uuid "b90c7511-6cb7-52d0-a241-3716bc5adacd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3af9f986-2d42-5c2c-92db-2115b25b8b1d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c13be24b-9c27-5e28-a002-16e6cff5ffdc" "answer-2dc3f69e-98b2-53fa-8063-59aefa158407" "answer-3af9f986-2d42-5c2c-92db-2115b25b8b1d" "answer-05595157-cac2-5680-a2b2-64951fa393e3" "answer-64a8af15-f8b3-5848-a8ef-a51af4a1f066"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c13be24b-9c27-5e28-a002-16e6cff5ffdc" :answer/id #uuid "c13be24b-9c27-5e28-a002-16e6cff5ffdc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{120}{(s+7)^{6}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2dc3f69e-98b2-53fa-8063-59aefa158407" :answer/id #uuid "2dc3f69e-98b2-53fa-8063-59aefa158407" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{120}{(s-6)^{6}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3af9f986-2d42-5c2c-92db-2115b25b8b1d" :answer/id #uuid "3af9f986-2d42-5c2c-92db-2115b25b8b1d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{120}{(s-7)^{6}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-05595157-cac2-5680-a2b2-64951fa393e3" :answer/id #uuid "05595157-cac2-5680-a2b2-64951fa393e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{60}{(s+7)^{6}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-64a8af15-f8b3-5848-a8ef-a51af4a1f066" :answer/id #uuid "64a8af15-f8b3-5848-a8ef-a51af4a1f066" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{60}{(s-7)^{6}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221946 :question/problem "Given that $L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},$ find the Laplace transform $L\\{{e}^{-4t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{7}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-4t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)\\} & =\\frac{s+4}{(s+4)^{2}+{7}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{s+4}{(s+4)^{2}+49}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186220284 :answer-field/correct "answer-3d88aee1-4ce0-5282-adf3-87f950f56337" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3d88aee1-4ce0-5282-adf3-87f950f56337" "answer-2cc22650-741c-5792-87dd-c71550abbc25" "answer-4f4907c7-8c42-50dc-b370-5907d5a52206" "answer-1bc22d0e-6715-5dd0-ab8e-b518e61d3e0e" "answer-dde48221-516d-591b-9446-b360359d4e9a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d88aee1-4ce0-5282-adf3-87f950f56337" :answer/id #uuid "3d88aee1-4ce0-5282-adf3-87f950f56337" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{s+4}{(s+4)^{2}+49}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2cc22650-741c-5792-87dd-c71550abbc25" :answer/id #uuid "2cc22650-741c-5792-87dd-c71550abbc25" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{s+4}{(s+4)^{2}+7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4f4907c7-8c42-50dc-b370-5907d5a52206" :answer/id #uuid "4f4907c7-8c42-50dc-b370-5907d5a52206" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{s+4}{(s-4)^{2}+49}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1bc22d0e-6715-5dd0-ab8e-b518e61d3e0e" :answer/id #uuid "1bc22d0e-6715-5dd0-ab8e-b518e61d3e0e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{s-4}{(s-4)^{2}+49}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dde48221-516d-591b-9446-b360359d4e9a" :answer/id #uuid "dde48221-516d-591b-9446-b360359d4e9a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{s}{(s+4)^{2}+49}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186304950 :question/problem "Given that  $L\\{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}}{({s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2})^{2}},$ find the Laplace transform $L\\{{e}^{4t}\\cdot t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\nL\\{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\}=\\frac{{s}^{2}-{3}^{2}}{({s}^{2}+{3}^{2})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{4t}\\cdot t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\} & =\\frac{(s-4)^{2}-{3}^{2}}{((s-4)^{2}+{3}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(s-4)^{2}-9}{((s-4)^{2}+9)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-4a4eb980-3664-5af2-945a-e7530601aa00" :question/id #uuid "4a4eb980-3664-5af2-945a-e7530601aa00" :question/math-academy-id "q-332174" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)=3{e}^{t}+4{t}^{2}{e}^{-3t}$  for $s>1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{3{e}^{t}+4{t}^{2}{e}^{-3t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{3{e}^{t}\\}+L\\{4{t}^{2}{e}^{-3t}\\} \\\\  & =3L\\{{e}^{t}\\}+4L\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{-3t}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{2}\\} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$then$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{-3t}\\} & =\\frac{2}{(s+3)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =3L\\{{e}^{t}\\}+4L\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{-3t}\\} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-1}+4\\cdot \\frac{2}{(s+3)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{s-1}+\\frac{8}{(s+3)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0f73d16d-36f0-5d60-9f4e-d0a31d53ec05"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0f73d16d-36f0-5d60-9f4e-d0a31d53ec05" :answer-field/id #uuid "0f73d16d-36f0-5d60-9f4e-d0a31d53ec05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-285676da-4135-583d-8899-d90d10d720ee" :answer-field/choices ["answer-226789de-c6c7-5856-86e1-65ea12a70479" "answer-01504f10-66fd-598a-98b7-004911e742cc" "answer-f1f0bbc4-71bc-5a80-9a09-af07edb9ffe6" "answer-285676da-4135-583d-8899-d90d10d720ee" "answer-741a34dc-db4b-5f21-90b7-c70416fe805d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-226789de-c6c7-5856-86e1-65ea12a70479" :answer/id #uuid "226789de-c6c7-5856-86e1-65ea12a70479" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s-1}+\\frac{8}{(s+3)^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-01504f10-66fd-598a-98b7-004911e742cc" :answer/id #uuid "01504f10-66fd-598a-98b7-004911e742cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{s+1}+\\frac{8}{(s+3)^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f1f0bbc4-71bc-5a80-9a09-af07edb9ffe6" :answer/id #uuid "f1f0bbc4-71bc-5a80-9a09-af07edb9ffe6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{s-1}+\\frac{4}{(s+3)^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-285676da-4135-583d-8899-d90d10d720ee" :answer/id #uuid "285676da-4135-583d-8899-d90d10d720ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{s-1}+\\frac{8}{(s+3)^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-741a34dc-db4b-5f21-90b7-c70416fe805d" :answer/id #uuid "741a34dc-db4b-5f21-90b7-c70416fe805d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{s-1}+\\frac{8}{(s-3)^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186368095 :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of  $f(t)={e}^{-4t}+5{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}$ for $s>3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{{e}^{-4t}+5{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{e}^{-4t}\\}+L\\{5{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{e}^{-4t}\\}+5L\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{2}\\} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$ then $L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\} & =\\frac{2}{(s-3)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{{e}^{-4t}\\}+5L\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+4}+5\\cdot \\frac{2}{(s-3)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+4}+\\frac{10}{(s-3)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>3.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186305000 :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of  $f(t)=6{e}^{2t}+2{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}$ for $s>2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{6{e}^{2t}+2{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{6{e}^{2t}\\}+L\\{2{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}\\} \\\\  & =6L\\{{e}^{2t}\\}+2L\\{{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{3}\\} & =\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$ then $L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}\\} & =\\frac{6}{(s+4)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =6L\\{{e}^{2t}\\}+2L\\{{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}\\} \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-2}+2\\cdot \\frac{6}{(s+4)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{s-2}+\\frac{12}{(s+4)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186304995 :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{4t}+5{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}$ for $s>4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{{e}^{4t}+5{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{e}^{4t}\\}+L\\{5{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{e}^{4t}\\}+5L\\{{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{3}\\} & =\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$ then $L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}\\} & =\\frac{6}{(s+1)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{{e}^{4t}\\}+5L\\{{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-4}+5\\cdot \\frac{6}{(s+1)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-4}+\\frac{30}{(s+1)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>4.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-b26511a8-09e9-54a7-8467-ef5beee0c7a0" :question/id #uuid "b26511a8-09e9-54a7-8467-ef5beee0c7a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-64307" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)=t({e}^{2t}-1)$ for $s>2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{t({e}^{2t}-1)\\} \\\\  & =L\\{t{e}^{2t}-t\\} \\\\  & =L\\{t{e}^{2t}\\}-L\\{t\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{t\\} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$ then $L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nL\\{t{e}^{2t}\\}=\\frac{1}{(s-2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{t{e}^{2t}\\}-L\\{t\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(s-2)^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(s-1)}{{s}^{2}(s-2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-15a092a7-1df9-5b92-bae4-dac2a2473380"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-15a092a7-1df9-5b92-bae4-dac2a2473380" :answer-field/id #uuid "15a092a7-1df9-5b92-bae4-dac2a2473380" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-071f7d36-b5bd-5169-8b91-30b9b8f89740" :answer-field/choices ["answer-36bc25f3-49a1-5de6-b0c1-7362aa7b3d17" "answer-111d64d2-13e2-51be-bf85-4c204ca01df3" "answer-071f7d36-b5bd-5169-8b91-30b9b8f89740" "answer-87ad7f9c-8811-5c95-b910-5eb165b987c9" "answer-58987a2a-0210-5329-9024-4178a6b1aae5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-36bc25f3-49a1-5de6-b0c1-7362aa7b3d17" :answer/id #uuid "36bc25f3-49a1-5de6-b0c1-7362aa7b3d17" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2s+1}{{s}^{2}(s-2)^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-111d64d2-13e2-51be-bf85-4c204ca01df3" :answer/id #uuid "111d64d2-13e2-51be-bf85-4c204ca01df3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2s-1}{{s}^{2}(s-2)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-071f7d36-b5bd-5169-8b91-30b9b8f89740" :answer/id #uuid "071f7d36-b5bd-5169-8b91-30b9b8f89740" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4(s-1)}{{s}^{2}(s-2)^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-87ad7f9c-8811-5c95-b910-5eb165b987c9" :answer/id #uuid "87ad7f9c-8811-5c95-b910-5eb165b987c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{s-1}{s(s-2)^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-58987a2a-0210-5329-9024-4178a6b1aae5" :answer/id #uuid "58987a2a-0210-5329-9024-4178a6b1aae5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{s}^{2}+2}{{s}^{2}(s-2)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186368081 :question/problem "![](images/82/82d01729627b5871739120d2e512cf54610eaaf43f6f2f2c454b7e39803937a2.png)\n\nConsider a function $f(t)$ for which the graph of the Laplace transform $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $L\\{{e}^{-2t}f(t)\\}?$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L\\{{e}^{-2t}f(t)\\},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $2$ units to the left.\n\n![](images/a9/a902bac22ba86af8de8b49d5aba5bb34f35433230f303432e1f4327708f41a7d.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221947 :question/problem "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s).$ How can we obtain the graph of $L\\{{e}^{-2t}f(t)\\}$ from the graph of $F(s)?$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L\\{{e}^{-2t}f(t)\\},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $2$ units to the left." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186220290 :answer-field/correct 17592186220294 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221930 :question/problem "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s).$ How can we obtain the graph of $L\\{{e}^{3t}f(t)\\}$ from the graph of $F(s)?$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L\\{{e}^{3t}f(t)\\},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $3$ units to the right." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186391762 :question/problem "![](images/ac/ac72ad6572e3dd3b9b75c5d7a681ab14df055031cf3b1baa3c5b0df2de7ae5a4.png)\n\nConsider a function $f(t)$ for which the graph of the Laplace transform $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $L\\{{e}^{-6t}f(t)\\}?$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L\\{{e}^{-6t}f(t)\\},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $6$ units to the left.\n\n![](images/fe/fe5d17ffdb189defcd001b26eadf333df4d19157af44beeaa4c270083d1a625d.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186304979 :question/problem "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s).$ How can we obtain the graph of $L\\{{e}^{5t}f(t)\\}$ from the graph of $F(s)?$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L\\{{e}^{5t}f(t)\\},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $5$ units to the right." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-526e692c-07a8-5c67-8d8e-821cf2ff406c" :question/id #uuid "526e692c-07a8-5c67-8d8e-821cf2ff406c" :question/math-academy-id "q-331797" :question/problem "Consider a function $z(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L\\{z(t)\\}=Z(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{e}^{-5t}z(t)\\}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-5t}z(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,z(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-5t}z(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-5t}\\,z(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+5)t}\\,z(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $Z(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,z(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+5)t}\\,z(t)\\,\\text{d}t=Z(s+5),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns+5>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}-5.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-258bced0-5662-54ae-9abc-a8aa92bf71d0" "answer-field-ef7b2b3