 #uuid "bedb9a68-3280-5cc1-b919-84f9a190864e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "s>{s}_{0}+5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-72493485-0552-5c95-bfee-670309f34738" :answer/id #uuid "72493485-0552-5c95-bfee-670309f34738" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "s>{s}_{0}-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-593b9890-14ef-5ad1-b4d2-54793d0a7bd4" :question/id #uuid "593b9890-14ef-5ad1-b4d2-54793d0a7bd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-331804" :question/problem "Consider a function $n(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L\\{n(t)\\}=N(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{e}^{-4t}n(t)\\}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-4t}n(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,n(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-4t}n(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-4t}\\,n(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+4)t}\\,n(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $N(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,n(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+4)t}\\,n(t)\\,\\text{d}t=N(s+4),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns+4>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}-4.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-96e21056-e8ae-5ed7-a152-1cefbfa4bc81" "answer-field-0d631dfa-ec45-5028-a991-072e67f0761d" "answer-field-dc08b231-e40a-5e8d-8f0a-dd2d12c01d08" "answer-field-71bbe0e4-0cd0-5cde-8680-91e8075f5bb4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-96e21056-e8ae-5ed7-a152-1cefbfa4bc81" :answer-field/id #uuid "96e21056-e8ae-5ed7-a152-1cefbfa4bc81" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/typ