ure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5628498d-d8a4-5ed6-9920-b0775ca8252a" :answer/id #uuid "5628498d-d8a4-5ed6-9920-b0775ca8252a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "s>{s}_{0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3e1d29dd-0da1-5a4e-a0b8-d795fce878ac" :answer/id #uuid "3e1d29dd-0da1-5a4e-a0b8-d795fce878ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "s>{s}_{0}+4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-66e268a9-ee32-5572-8e3b-5a323ac128b3" :answer/id #uuid "66e268a9-ee32-5572-8e3b-5a323ac128b3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "s>{s}_{0}-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e41ff26b-731d-5b74-9b6c-2d6a197328e1" :question/id #uuid "e41ff26b-731d-5b74-9b6c-2d6a197328e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-331790" :question/problem "Consider a function $u(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L\\{u(t)\\}=U(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{e}^{-2t}u(t)\\}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-2t}u(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,u(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-2t}u(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-2t}\\,u(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+2)t}\\,u(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $U(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,u(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+2)t}\\,u(t)\\,\\text{d}t=U(s+2),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns+2>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}-2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e9d61a84-8021-5538-a619-9cfff81bd6a2" "answer-field-1fdbb054-76b6-5d8e-bad7-c502b9102a3c" "answer-field