"field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-8678ec39-612d-5d12-8aab-d0b956ae984e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c0fd92a1-c142-5dff-853f-82f272ebcd4b" "answer-dec0ba2e-d56c-59e5-94a7-cd5b570783f8" "answer-8678ec39-612d-5d12-8aab-d0b956ae984e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c0fd92a1-c142-5dff-853f-82f272ebcd4b" :answer/id #uuid "c0fd92a1-c142-5dff-853f-82f272ebcd4b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "s>{s}_{0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dec0ba2e-d56c-59e5-94a7-cd5b570783f8" :answer/id #uuid "dec0ba2e-d56c-59e5-94a7-cd5b570783f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "s>{s}_{0}+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8678ec39-612d-5d12-8aab-d0b956ae984e" :answer/id #uuid "8678ec39-612d-5d12-8aab-d0b956ae984e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "s>{s}_{0}-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186222476 :tutorial/content "In a previous lesson, we learned how to compute Laplace transforms. We now examine how the Laplace transform changes when a function is multiplied by an exponential.\n\nSuppose\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)\n$$\n\nexists for $s>s_0.$ We want to compute the Laplace transform of $e^{at}f(t).$\n\nStarting from the definition of the Laplace transform,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,{e}^{at}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-a)t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-st}\\,f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t,\n\\qquad s>s_0.\n$$\n\nComparing the two integrals, we see that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-(s-a)t}\\,f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t\n=F(s-a).\n$$\n\nFor this to make sense, we need $s-a>s_0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\ns>s_0+a.\n$$\n\nThis result is known as the **First Shifting Theorem**:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{at}f(t)\\} = F(s-a).\n$$\n\nIn the next slide, we will apply this result to a concrete example." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186222509 :tutorial/content "The **First shifting Theorem** states that if\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)\n$$\n\nfor $s>s_0$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{e^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a),\n\\qquad s>s_0+a.\n$$\n\nSo, when you multiply a function by $e^{at}$, you do two things:\n\n- *Replace* $s$ with $s-a$ in the known transform $F(s)$.\n\n- *Shift the domain* from $s>s_0$ to $s>s_0+a$.\n\nTo see this in action, consider the following example.\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{\\sin(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{\\omega}{s^2+\\omega^2},\n\\qquad s>0.\n$$\n\nSuppose we want to compute\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{2t}\\sin(3t)\\}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we identify the base transform:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{\\sin(3t)\\}=\\frac{3}{s^2+3^2}\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the first shifting theorem with $a=2$, which means we replace $s$ by $s-2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\} & =\\frac{3}{(s-2)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(s-2)^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the original transform is valid for $s>0$, the shifted transform is valid for\n\n$$\ns>2.\n$$\n\nThis example illustrates the general pattern: multiplying by $e^{at}$ leads to a simple substitution $s:= s-a$, along with a corresponding shift in the domain." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221462 :activity/expected-seconds 1200.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
